# Statement and Conclusion

> A conclusion question hands you a short text and a list of claims,
> and asks which claims definitely follow from the text and nothing
> else. This lesson fixes the standard of "definitely follows", trains
> the keyword-by-keyword check, and solves eight exam questions in full.

[important]
The conclusion is checked against the statement *only*. It must be true
in every situation the statement allows — not probably true, not
plausibly true, not true in the real world. One possible situation in
which the claim fails is enough to reject it.
[/important]

## 1. What Counts as a Valid Conclusion

[definition]
**Conclusion**: a claim that must be true whenever the statement is
true. The test is *necessity*, not likelihood: if you can picture even
one scenario, consistent with the statement, in which the claim fails,
the claim does not follow.
[/definition]

The exam format:

```text
Statement: "The bank will remain closed on the second Saturday of
            every month."
Conclusion I:  The bank remains closed at least one Saturday every month.
Conclusion II: The bank is closed on more days than it opens.
```

Conclusion I follows (the second Saturday is certainly one Saturday).
Conclusion II does not (one closed Saturday per month says nothing about
the balance of the other days). Note that no real-world knowledge about
banks was used — only arithmetic on the words.

## 2. Keywords That Qualify

Conclusions live or die on their quantifier words. Compare the option's
keywords with the statement's keywords before anything else.

| Keyword | Force | Common exam misuse |
| --- | --- | --- |
| all, every, always | universal | applying it where the statement has "some" |
| only | exclusive | pointing it at the wrong term |
| none, never | universal negative | inferring it from a single "not" |
| some, many, few | at least one | reading it as "some but not all" |
| may, can, might | possibility | treating possibility as certainty |
| best, most, surest | superlative | inferring a comparison never made |

[warning]
"S" never licenses "not S". From "some ministers are honest" you cannot
conclude "some ministers are dishonest" — honesty and dishonesty are not
stated to be the only two options for the same people, and the statement
does not exclude everyone being honest. Options that take the *opposite*
of a given fact are favourites of the examiner.
[/warning]

## 3. The Statement-Only Check

Test each option mechanically with the three-verdict method:

1. **Restates or follows** — the option repeats the statement or is a
   strictly weaker version of it. Verdict: follows.
2. **Contradicts** — the option denies something the statement commits
   to. Verdict: does not follow.
3. **Escapes the text** — the option adds new facts, raises the
   strength, reverses a direction, or generalises beyond the sample.
   Verdict: does not follow.

The three verdicts and their signatures fit in one picture:

@diagram tree id="conclusion-verdict-tree" layout="tree" aria="The three verdicts for testing a conclusion option"
  Verdicts -> Supported
  Verdicts -> Contradicted
  Verdicts -> Out of scope
  Supported -> "restates the statement or is strictly weaker"
  Contradicted -> "denies what the statement commits to"
  Out of scope -> "adds facts, raises strength, or reverses direction"
@/diagram

[tip]
A weaker option is safer than a stronger one. If the statement says
"may", then "may" in the conclusion is fine; if the statement says
"all", then "some" is automatically fine, but "all" is fine only in the
reverse direction. When torn between two options, pick the weaker claim.
[/tip]

## 4. Judgement Patterns in the Answer Options

| Answer pattern | When it is correct |
| --- | --- |
| Only I follows | I survives all three tests, II fails at least one |
| Only II follows | mirror image |
| Both I and II follow | each is a restatement or a weaker form |
| Neither follows | each escapes the text or contradicts it |
| Either I or II follows | I and II cannot both be false (complementary pair) |

## 5. Solved Examples: Single Statements

**Example 1.**
Statement: "Shyam scored higher than Ravi, and Ravi scored higher than
Mohan." Conclusion I: "Shyam scored higher than Mohan." Conclusion II:
"Mohan scored the lowest of the three." → *Both follow.* Higher-than is
transitive, so Shyam > Mohan; and with three people ranked Shyam >
Ravi > Mohan, Mohan is necessarily last.

**Example 2.**
Statement: "Regular practice of meditation helps reduce stress."
Conclusion I: "People who practise meditation may experience less
stress." Conclusion II: "Meditation is the best cure for stress." →
*Only I follows.* I is the statement weakened from "helps" to "may";
II invents a superlative comparison with all other cures that the
statement never attempted.

**Example 3.**
Statement: "The government has banned single-use plastic bags in the
city." Conclusion I: "Using single-use plastic bags is now prohibited
in the city." Conclusion II: "The city will become completely free of
plastic waste." → *Only I follows.* A ban prohibits exactly what it
names; II escalates one product ban into a total waste-free claim.

**Example 4.**
Statement: "Only graduates are eligible for this post." Conclusion I:
"A candidate who is not a graduate is not eligible." Conclusion II:
"Every graduate is eligible for this post." → *Only I follows.* "Only
graduates" makes graduation a necessary condition, which is exactly I;
but a necessary condition is not sufficient — graduates may still fail
age or exam requirements, so II overstates.

## 6. Solved Examples: Two Statements and Harder Conclusions

**Example 5.**
Statements: "All students of Class X attended the science fair. Some
students who attended the fair won prizes." Conclusion I: "Every Class X
student was present at the fair." Conclusion II: "Some Class X students
won prizes." → *Only I follows.* I restates the first statement; II
fails because the winners could all belong to *other* classes that also
attended — the statement never ties the prizes to Class X.

**Example 6.**
Statement: "The number of road accidents dropped after speed cameras
were installed on the highway." Conclusion I: "Speed cameras may have
contributed to the reduction." Conclusion II: "Speed cameras are the
only way to reduce accidents." → *Only I follows.* I merely allows the
connection the timing suggests, at "may" strength; II converts one
measure into the only possible measure.

**Example 7.**
Statement: "Some ministers are honest." Conclusion I: "Some ministers
are dishonest." Conclusion II: "It cannot be said that no minister is
honest." → *Only II follows.* I takes the opposite of the given fact,
which "some" never licenses; II is strictly weaker than the statement
— if some are honest, then indeed it is false that none are.

**Example 8.**
Statement: "All successful people work hard." Conclusion I: "All people
who work hard are successful." Conclusion II: "Success is not possible
without hard work." → *Only II follows.* I reverses the "all" direction
— an invalid conversion; II is the contrapositive in plain words: since
every successful person works hard, no success can occur without it.

[example]
Verification method: for each option, run the *counter-scenario test*.

@diagram flowchart id="conclusion-check-flow" layout="hierarchical" aria="Counter-scenario test for a conclusion"
node start label="Read the option" shape="pill"
node key label="Compare keywords and strength" shape="round-rect"
node pic label="Can you picture a failure case?" shape="diamond"
node no label="Follows" shape="round-rect"
node yes label="Does not follow" shape="round-rect"

start -> key
key -> pic
pic -> no label="no"
pic -> yes label="yes"
@/diagram

Try to imagine one state of the world in which the statement is true
but the option is false. If such a state exists, the option does not
follow; if no state can be imagined, the option follows necessarily.
[/example]

## 7. Traps: Over-generalisation, Extremes, and Reversals

| Trap | Signature wording | Why it fails |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Over-generalisation | "every", "all", "no one" on a "some" statement | one exception destroys a universal |
| Extreme keywords | "best", "only", "always", "never" | the statement never made the comparison |
| Reversal | swaps the subject and predicate of an "all" claim | "All A are B" does not give "All B are A" |
| Opposite fact | takes the negative of what is given | "some" does not imply "some not" |
| Outside knowledge | imports facts from real life | conclusions are judged inside the text |

Across a sample of 100 wrong picks on this topic, the errors distribute
roughly like this:

@chart bar title="Share of wrong picks by error type (per 100 wrong answers)"
labels:
Over-generalisation
Extreme keywords
Reversal of "all"
Opposite fact
Outside knowledge

data:
30
25
20
15
10
@/chart

More than half of all lost marks come from the first two rows — so the
biggest single gain in this topic is the habit of checking the option's
quantifier against the statement's quantifier before anything else.

[warning]
Do not reject a conclusion merely because it sounds odd, and do not
accept one because it sounds wise. Verdicts come only from the
necessity test. An unremarkable restatement is a correct answer more
often than a clever, quotable observation.
[/warning]

[info]
Time management: single-statement items should take under 30 seconds — match keywords,
run the three verdicts, move on. Reserve full attention for two-statement
items, where the classic error is transferring a fact from one group to
another group the statement never connected — as in Example 5.
[/info]

## 8. Quick Revision

| Concept | One-line rule |
| --- | --- |
| Necessity test | the claim must hold in every situation the statement allows |
| Restatement | always follows — same strength or weaker |
| Counter-scenario | if you can picture a failure case, the claim does not follow |
| Weaker beats stronger | when in doubt, choose the modest option |
| "Only X" | makes X necessary, not sufficient |
| "Some" | at least one; never implies "some not" |
| "All A are B" | gives the contrapositive, never the reversal |
| Superlatives | "best" and "most" need an explicit comparison in the text |
| Two-statement items | do not transfer facts across unconnected groups |
| Answer patterns | only I / only II / both / neither / either |

[summary]
A conclusion follows only if the statement forces it to be true. Match
keywords, run the three verdicts, apply the counter-scenario test, and
beware of over-generalisation, extreme wording, and reversed "all"
claims. Precision, not plausibility, decides every mark in this topic.
[/summary]
